AB  - ABSTRACT Salmonella enterica remains a major pathogen in poultry, the intestinal colonization of which contributes to the contamination of meat and eggs. This study evaluated edible biopolymeric microcapsular wrappings (EBMW) as “Trojan-horse” carriers for two lytic bacteriophages targeting S. enterica. Chitosan-coated calcium alginate microparticles were produced by internal gelation and characterized for phage viability, protection in simulated gastric (pH 2.5) and intestinal (pH 6.8) fluids, and antibacterial efficacy under ex vivo conditions using chicken intestinal segments. Encapsulation preserved phage lytic activity and significantly delayed inactivation under acidic conditions, maintaining viable titers for 45-60 min compared to rapid loss in free phages. In ex vivo assays, EBMW-encapsulated phages achieved up to 6-log reduction of S. enterica in the ileum-ceca segment, with higher efficacy at a multiplicity of infection (MOI) of 1000 than of 100. Phage titers remained stable or increased, indicating active replication and sustained release. These findings demonstrate that EBMW provide a protective, time-controlled delivery system for oral phage administration. The segment-specific efficacy, especially in the ceca-the main Salmonella reservoir in poultry-highlights the potential of EBMW-encapsulated phages as an effective biocontrol strategy for reducing Salmonella loads in poultry production.
